229 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Operation Principle of Deep Image Prior \(DIP\))
/E (Operation Principle of Deep Image Prior \(DIP\))
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
230 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (1. No Training Data Required Unlike traditional deep learning methods that require massive datasets, DIP operates on a single corrupted image.  2.Network Structure as the Prior  The core idea is that the structure of a CNN itself acts as a "prior." CNNs are naturally good at generating natural image statistics but are resistant to learning random noise. 3. The Optimization Process The process starts with a randomly initialized network and a fixed random noise input. The network is trained to reproduce the corrupted target image. 4. Early Stopping Since the network learns meaningful structures \(the clean image\) much faster than it memorizes noise, we stop the training process before it overfits to the corruption. The result at this intermediate step is the restored, clean image.)
/E (1. No Training Data Required Unlike traditional deep learning methods that require massive datasets, DIP operates on a single corrupted image.  2.Network Structure as the Prior  The core idea is that the structure of a CNN itself acts as a "prior." CNNs are naturally good at generating natural image statistics but are resistant to learning random noise. 3. The Optimization Process The process starts with a randomly initialized network and a fixed random noise input. The network is trained to reproduce the corrupted target image. 4. Early Stopping Since the network learns meaningful structures \(the clean image\) much faster than it memorizes noise, we stop the training process before it overfits to the corruption. The result at this intermediate step is the restored, clean image.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
